colorful scenes of pure outraged  businesses being evicted after decades  in the neighborhood  elderly citizens being pushed from the  only homes they've ever known and  payoffs to union and community activists  Gratton Iran signed a community benefits  agreement with the city and with I think  eight community groups six of which  weren't even established groups before  the Atlantic Yards project was announced  and so they were supposedly negotiating  benefits for this community but  led to questions who did they represent  if they weren't an established group  already it came out later that they had  been paid and more being paid by Forest  City Ratner and even at this point there  aren't any jobs and guild as the you  know the group representing jobs but  they're funded by the developer so how  are they gonna how they're gonna fight  back if they say hey you know we need  jobs they their funding comes in the  developer so they can't some of the  writers have said it kind of paints the  government and the developer as you know  in bed together well they are the story  is so corrupt it's so corrupt it's  really hard to imagine how corrupt it is  when you know what's going on this kind  of process of top-down government is  endemic in New York it's epidemic the  whole Bloomberg model goes back to the  Moses model which we all know created  some benefits but incredible impacts as  well that neighborhoods are still have  not recovered from we do Q&amp;A is at many  screenings we've been to festivals in  Canada and we showed a rough-cut  screening in Dallas and everywhere we  show it there is someone in the audience  who says that's just like what's  happening in Vancouver with the Olympics  well that's just like what happened with  Texas Stadium or you know someone will  also always relate it somehow to their  lives when they see the film you can't  build a community you can build a series  of buildings but it takes a long time  for community to form and community has  roots I mean you pull up a community by  its roots that community goes into root  shock the results of that are all kinds  of negative consequences for America I  mean the whole history of urban renewal  is is a nightmare  and yet liberal people still tend to  think that's a good idea because we're  building housing but you can't look at  benefits without looking at the  consequences since the project has kind  of pushed forward it has really not  created jobs and even the big supporters  of the project have come to us and said  you know the movie is very fair it had  seemed to you know divided on  ideological dividing lines when that  really made no sense at all because  people were conservative like the movie  people who are liberal like the movie  people who are in-between like the movie  because you know our tagline knows that  everybody hates kleptocracy everybody  hates it when the government is working  in junction with a favored partner to  enrich both of them  it's it's it's unamerican but what to  make of this is it's a movie  much like Erin Brockovich it follows a  character fighting in injustice  [Music]
 

